  "panel_reasoning_excerpt": "## TEMPLATE 15: PP_CULTURAL_PRIOR_CALIBRATION_001\n**\"Cultural visual ecology \u2192 generative model calibration \u2192 preference set-points\"**\n\n**Structural pattern**: developmental_exposure \u2192 model_calibration \u2192 shifted_optimization_zone\n**Higher-order principle**: The Goldilocks framework for cross-cultural variation \u2014 the inverted-U curve has the same shape but is SHIFTED along the stimulus dimension based on cultural experience\n\n**Causal chain**:\n1. Developmental and ongoing exposure to cultural visual environment (sensory density of markets, complexity of architectural ornament, visual density of public spaces) \u2192 statistical learning in visual generative model\n   - Level: ecological \u2192 computational\n   - Bridging quality: MEDIUM\n   - Maturity: **how-plausibly** (theoretical derivation from PP + statistical learning literature; cross-cultural application is novel)\n2. Calibrated generative model \u2192 shifted prediction error function (stimuli that produce moderate prediction error in Culture A produce low prediction error in Culture B, if Culture B has higher baseline sensory density)\n   - Level: computational\n   - Bridging quality: MEDIUM\n   - Maturity: **how-possibly** (this is the central theoretical claim of the Goldilocks cultural framework \u2014 it has not been directly tested)\n3. Shifted prediction error function \u2192 shifted complexity preference \u2192 Culture B prefers higher complexity than Culture A, at levels that Culture A finds overwhelming\n   - Level: computational \u2192 psychological\n   - Bridging quality: MEDIUM\n   - Maturity: **how-possibly**\n\n**Scope conditions**: Applies to any sensory dimension where cultural experience varies systematically. Best established for visual complexity; should extend to auditory (music complexity preferences), olfactory (spice tolerance), thermal (comfort range), social (density tolerance). Developmental sensitive period: likely strongest during childhood visual environment exposure, but ongoing calibration occurs throughout life.\n**Known interactions**: This template EXPLAINS the empirical finding (if confirmed) that Indian sensory set-points are elevated across modalities \u2014 it's not a cultural \"preference\" but a calibrated generative model that processes higher sensory intensity as moderate rather than overwhelming.\n**Overall maturity**: **how-possibly** \u2014 the most speculative template in the initial library, but it generates highly specific, testable predictions central to the India fieldwork\n\n**Key references**: Clark (2013, cited ~5,500); Friston (2010, cited ~8,000); [India fieldwork program \u2014 forthcoming]\n\n---",
